      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 3, 4, 6, and 7. The LCM of these numbers will give us the number of school days after which Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the lab.\n\n- Multiples of 3 up to 30: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 30\n- Multiples of 4 up to 30: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28\n- Multiples of 6 up to 30: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30\n- Multiples of 7 up to 30: 7, 14, 21, 28\n\nThe least common multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 7 is 84. This means that after 84 school days, Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi will next be together tutoring in the lab. So the answer is \\boxed{84}.\n\nTo verify this, we can check a few days after 84 to ensure that the condition is met. For example, after 84 school days, Darren will have worked every third day, having been in the lab on days \\[3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27,30,33,36,39,42,45,48,51,54,57,60,63,66,69,72,75,78,81,84\\]. Wanda will have worked every fourth day, having been in the lab on days \\[4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,40,44,48,52,56,60,64,68,72,76,80,84\\]. Beatrice will have worked every sixth day, having been in the lab on days \\[6,12,18,24,30,36,42,48,54,60,66,72,78,84\\]. Chi will have worked every seventh day, having been in the lab on days \\[7,14,21,28,35,42,49,56,63,70,77,84\\]. It can be seen that after 84 school days, the numbers on the lab tutoring cycles ( Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi ) align.\n\nHence, \\(\\boxed{84}\\) school days from today will be the next time that Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi will be together tutoring in the lab.\nThe code to verify this is provided below:\n\n```python\n# Calculate the least common multiple (LCM) of 3, 4, 6, and 7\nimport math\n\n# Function to calculate LCM using GCD\ndef lcm(a, b):\n    return abs(a * b) // math.gcd(a, b)\n\nlcm_3_4 = lcm(3, 4)\nlcm_3_4_6 = lcm(lcm_3_4, 6)\nlcm_3_4_6_7 = lcm(lcm_3_4_6, 7)\n\n# Least common multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 7\nprint(lcm_3_4_6_7)\n```",
