      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let's determine the least common multiple (LCM) of 3, 4, 6, and 7 to find out when all of them will be working together at the same time.\n\nThe first step is to determine all the prime factors of each number1:\n\n- 3 = 3\n- 4 = 2²\n- 6 = 2 × 3\n- 7 = 7\n\nTo find the LCM, you take 2 simultaneously raised to its maximum exponent from each place factor, and then multiply each of these powers by the respective the number for each entry. For 2, the minimum exponent of 2 is 2 (from 4). For 3, the minimum exponent of 3 is 1 (from 3 and 6). For 7, the minimum exponent of 7 is 1 since 7 only occurs once. Therefore:\n\nLCM = 2² × 3 × 7 = 4 × 3 × 7 = 4 × 21 = 84.\n\nThe Least Common Multiple, or LCM, of Darren, Wanda, Beatrice, and Chi is 84.\n\nTherefore, the next time they will all be working together in the lab is in 84 school days.",
